---
intent: >
  Ship aide_brain — the runtime brain entry-point MCP tool — composing the
  lifted buildBrainState helper (fixed brain MCP key, command/args-shape
  backend matching) with the new brainBackends registry to return
  { status, backend, instructions } where instructions is server-assembled,
  ready-to-execute prose telling the agent which mcp__brain__* tools to
  use to reach the wired backend's seeded entry-point file, with non-empty
  remediation prose on every non-ok branch.
---

## Project Structure

After this plan executes, `src/tools/brain/` contains a thin orchestrator
that composes the (prerequisite) lifted `buildBrainState` helper with the
(prerequisite) `brainBackends` registry, plus a single in-module helper
that turns a resolved `BrainState` into the server-assembled prose. The
tool is wired into `src/index.ts` as a peer of `aide_info`.

The tree below is module-scoped to `src/tools/brain/`. Files outside
this directory that this plan touches (`src/index.ts`, `src/types/index.ts`,
`src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts`) are declared as explicit
cross-cutting edits in the steps below; they are not part of this module's
folder tree.

```
src/tools/brain/
├── .aide                              ← [existing] intent spec for this orchestrator
├── plan.aide                          ← [this file] implementation plan
├── index.ts                           ← [new] orchestrator: brain(root: string) => Promise<BrainToolResult>
├── index.test.ts                      ← [new] co-located tests covering the three status branches and the registry-dispatch path
└── composeInstructions/               ← [new] helper: turn a resolved BrainState into the agent-facing prose
    ├── index.ts                       ← [new] composeInstructions(state: BrainState) => { backend: string | null, instructions: string }
    └── index.test.ts                  ← [new] co-located unit tests for the three prose branches (ok, no-mcp-entry, invalid-path)
```

The orchestrator's only job is: call `buildBrainState(root)` (prerequisite —
returns `BrainState` with the registry-resolved `backend`), pass the
resulting state to `composeInstructions`, and return
`{ status, backend, instructions }`. No backend detection logic lives
inside this module — that belongs to the lifted `buildBrainState` and
the `brainBackends` registry, both declared as prerequisites.

## Prerequisites

Two upstream modules must be built before this plan can execute. Each
has its own plan.aide produced separately by the architect. This plan
declares only the contracts it consumes.

### 1. `src/service/brainBackends/` — backend driver registry

The canonical-code module that owns per-backend identity inference and
entry-point reach prose. Maps an MCP entry's `command/args` shape to a
backend driver (today only `obsidian`; tomorrow `notion`, `github-wiki`,
etc.).

Must export at minimum:

- A default export `resolveBackend(entry: McpServerEntry): BackendDriver | null`
  where `McpServerEntry` is the shape under `mcpServers[<key>]` in
  `.mcp.json` (`{ command: string; args: string[] }`), and `BackendDriver`
  is `{ id: string; renderInstructions(state: { vaultPath: string }): string }`.
  Returns `null` when no matcher fires (unknown backend wired under the
  `brain` key).
- `BackendDriver` and `McpServerEntry` re-exported as types from
  `src/types/index.ts` so consumers (this plan, `buildBrainState`) reference
  them without reaching into the service module's internals.

The registry's matcher table, the obsidian matcher's command/args
predicate, and the obsidian instruction template all live inside this
prerequisite module. **This plan does NOT prescribe the registry's
internal structure** — only that the resolver-by-entry contract above is
honored.

The `renderInstructions(state)` contract: receives the resolved
`vaultPath` (and any other backend-relevant state the registry chooses
to require — but not the mode of failure; failure prose is the brain
tool's job, not the registry's). Returns the multi-sentence
ready-to-execute prose block per the spec's "ok" branch — names
`mcp__brain__read_note`, the path to the seeded entry-point, and the
closing "the brain takes over from here" sentence. The prose **must not**
duplicate folder structure, search semantics, or write rules — those
live in the seeded `CLAUDE.md`.

### 2. `src/service/buildBrainState/` — lifted brain-state detector

The existing `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/` lifted to a service
location with the new fixed-key + registry-matcher contract. The lift
is required because horizontal isolation forbids `src/tools/brain/`
from importing `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/` (sibling tool).

Must export `default async function buildBrainState(root: string): Promise<BrainState>`
where `BrainState` (extended in `src/types/index.ts` per step 2 below)
is the tagged union `{ status, vaultPath, hints, backend }`:

- Reads the host's framework-specific MCP config path via `detectFramework(root)`.
- Looks up the **fixed** `"brain"` key in `mcpServers` (no per-backend key
  scan).
- When the `brain` entry is present, hands the entry shape to the
  `brainBackends` registry's `resolveBackend(entry)`. When the registry
  returns a driver, populates `backend: <driver.id>`. When the registry
  returns `null` (unknown backend wired), populates `backend: null` and
  status falls through to `no-mcp-entry` (the user wired something the
  server doesn't recognize — same remediation as "not wired at all":
  re-run `/aide:brain config`).
- All existing edge cases (missing config, malformed JSON, missing key,
  malformed args, broken vault path, empty-string vault path) collapse
  to the same `BrainState` outcomes the original helper produced —
  with the addition of the new `backend` discriminant.
- All non-`ok` outcomes carry `backend: null` (no resolved driver for a
  branch that did not reach `ok`).
- `ok` carries `backend: <driver.id>` (the registry-resolved identifier).
- The `obsidian` key is **not** consulted as a fallback in this plan
  (per the migration decision below); the install service migrates
  existing `obsidian` keys to `brain` at upgrade time.

This module's plan.aide is a separate deliverable — the architect
produces it before this plan runs. The lift preserves all existing
test coverage of the five edge-case branches; the new contract adds
one additional branch (`brain` key present but registry returns
`null`) which gets its own test in the prerequisite's plan, not this one.

