           `{ backend: null, instructions: <NO_MCP_ENTRY_PROSE> }`.
           The prose must tell the agent (a) no brain backend is wired,
           (b) surface this to the user, (c) recommend running
           `/aide:brain config` to wire the brain, (d) instruct the
           agent not to proceed with the task as if the brain were
           available. Mirrors the spec's "no-mcp-entry" Good example
           verbatim in intent.
        2. `status === "invalid-path"` → return
           `{ backend: null, instructions: <INVALID_PATH_PROSE> }`.
           The prose must (a) name that a brain backend is configured
           but its vault path does not resolve on disk, (b) surface
           this to the user, (c) recommend running `/aide:brain config`
           to repoint the vault, (d) instruct the agent not to proceed
           with the task as if the brain were available. Mirrors the
           spec's "invalid-path" Good example verbatim in intent.
        3. `status === "ok"` → ask the registry to resolve the wired
           driver. Since `buildBrainState` already populated
           `state.backend` with the resolved driver id (per the
           prerequisite's contract), and `vaultPath` is non-null on
           `ok`, the helper passes `state.vaultPath` to the registry's
           `renderInstructions({ vaultPath })`. Return
           `{ backend: state.backend, instructions: <rendered prose> }`.
