## Decisions

**Migration path: rename `obsidian` to `brain` at install time
(option 1 from the prompt).** Rejected: tolerate both keys in
`buildBrainState` for a transition period (option 2). Why option 1
wins: keeping the runtime detection logic single-fixed-key keeps the
`buildBrainState` lift simple — no fallback branch, no transitional
state to test, no doc burden explaining "we look here first then
there." The cost lands on the install service exactly once (during
upgrade), and `applySteps`'s existing `would-overwrite` flow already
handles the agent-confirmation pattern this needs. Why not option 3
(defer migration entirely): existing installs would silently lose
their brain wiring on first run after this plan ships, which violates
the spec's "never silent" invariant. Migrating at install time means
existing users see a `would-overwrite` prompt during their next
`/aide:upgrade` or `/aide:init`, with the change surfaced explicitly.

**`backend` field on `BrainState` (not just on the tool result).**
Rejected: leave `BrainState` unchanged and have the brain tool re-call
the registry. Why the field belongs on `BrainState`: the registry
match happens once during detection (in `buildBrainState`) — re-doing
the match in the tool would either require the tool to import
`brainBackends` and the entry-shape extraction logic, or require a
second registry call after `buildBrainState` already had the entry in
hand. Carrying the resolved `backend` forward on `BrainState` makes
the tool's job pure formatting. The cost is one extra field on a
shared type — small relative to the boundary cleanliness. The field
is documented as "non-null on `ok`, `null` otherwise" via JSDoc on
the field rather than as a discriminated union variant — the existing
`status` discriminant is already load-bearing, and stacking a second
discriminant would force every consumer to narrow twice.

**`composeInstructions` is a helper subfolder, not inline in the
orchestrator.** Rejected: define `composeInstructions` as a private
function above the orchestrator's default export. Why subfolder:
progressive disclosure's aggressive-modularization rule — every
helper gets its own folder so it is independently importable and
testable. The helper's three-branch logic + defensive `vaultPath:
null` clause (step 5d) is enough behaviour to deserve isolated tests.
The orchestrator stays at a size where its inline pipeline narration
is the full story; the helper grows independently if more branches
arrive (e.g. a future `unsupported-backend` status).

**`composeInstructions` calls `resolveBackend` (the registry) instead
of receiving a pre-resolved driver via parameter.** Rejected: thread
the driver from `buildBrainState` through `BrainState.backend` as the
literal driver object (not just `id`). Why call the registry inside
the helper: serializing a driver function across the
`buildBrainState` → orchestrator → helper boundary couples the
detection layer's return shape to a runtime-only value (functions
don't serialize). Storing only the `id` on `BrainState` keeps the
state plain-data — the `composeInstructions` helper re-resolves the
driver from the `id` via the registry's `getDriverById(id)` accessor
or by re-running `resolveBackend(entry)`. The prerequisite plan for
`brainBackends` decides which accessor to expose; this plan consumes
either via a single registry call. **Note:** if the registry's plan
chooses to expose only `resolveBackend(entry)` (no by-id lookup),
this helper must reach for the entry shape itself, which means
`buildBrainState` must also surface the matched `entry` on
`BrainState`. That is a contract refinement to negotiate during the
registry's spec round — surfaced in `## Follow-ups`.

**`status` vocabulary stays identical to `aide_info.brain.status`.**
Rejected: introduce a new status like `unsupported-backend` for the
new "wired-but-no-matcher" branch. Why reuse: the spec's desired
outcome explicitly pins this — "an agent that already saw brain state
at boot does not learn new terms here." A wired-but-unmatched
backend collapses to `no-mcp-entry` (same remediation as
"never wired" — re-run `/aide:brain config` to re-wire to a
recognized backend). The `BackendDriver` registry's failure mode is
identical to "no entry" from the agent's perspective.

**Error handling: leaf tool layer never throws.** Rejected: let
errors propagate up to the MCP `CallToolRequestSchema` handler. Why
catch at the tool layer (via `buildBrainState`'s structured-state
contract): the spec's undesired outcomes forbid silent failure and
require non-empty `instructions` on every branch. A thrown error
from `buildBrainState` would surface as an MCP protocol error with
no structured `instructions`, defeating the spec. By design,
`buildBrainState` never throws (per the prerequisite's contract);
`composeInstructions` is pure and defends the impossible-state
branch (step 5d); the orchestrator therefore inherits "never throws"
by composition.

**No `.aide` spec next to `composeInstructions/`.** Per the
progressive-disclosure rule that `.aide` files live next to
orchestrator `index.ts` files only — helper folder names are
self-explanatory. The orchestrator's `.aide` (already written) is
the single source of truth for this module's intent.

**Orchestrator pipeline: detection → composition → return (linear,
not parallel).** Rejected: parallelize `buildBrainState` and any
other I/O. Why linear: there is no other I/O. The single async hop
is `buildBrainState`, which already runs detection and registry
lookup in sequence internally (per the prerequisite's contract).
The composition step is pure. Adding `Promise.all` here would be
ceremonial.

**`backend` field on the tool response, not stripped to status-only.**
Rejected: omit `backend` from the response and let agents parse the
prose for the identifier. Why keep `backend`: the spec's desired
outcome pins `backend` as a structured signal of which branch fired
— `null` vs. non-null lets the `/aide:brain` Mode 2 shim branch
programmatically without parsing prose. Stripping it would force
every consumer to regex the prose, which is exactly the
consumer-side parsing the spec's "structured fields forbidden"
undesired outcome was meant to prevent on the *input* side; the
*output* side wants the opposite — one structured discriminant
plus pre-formatted prose, in parallel.

## Follow-ups

The orchestrator running this plan must schedule these as separate
plans/tasks after this plan completes. They are dependents (consumers
of this plan's contract), not prerequisites, so they do not block
this plan from being built.

- **`src/service/brainBackends/` spec round.** This plan declares the
  registry's external contract in `## Prerequisites`, but the
  registry's internal structure (matcher table format, how `obsidian`'s
  matcher inspects `command/args`, where instruction templates live —
  inline strings vs. separate files, how new backends are registered)
  is its own architectural surface. Run `/aide:spec` on
  `src/service/brainBackends/` before its `plan.aide` is produced.
  Resolve during the spec round: whether the registry exposes only
  `resolveBackend(entry)` or also `getDriverById(id)` — see the
  Decisions note on `composeInstructions` for the negotiation point.

- **`src/service/buildBrainState/` plan round.** The lift from
  `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/` plus the new fixed-key + registry
  matcher contract is its own architect deliverable. Existing tests
  must move with the source; one new test branch is added (`brain`
  key present but registry returns `null` → `no-mcp-entry`,
  `backend: null`).

- **`/aide:brain` Mode 2 shim refactor (`.claude/commands/aide/brain.md`).**
  Today the shim duplicates Obsidian-specific prose from the prior
  command. Once `aide_brain` ships, the shim collapses to "call
  `aide_brain`, branch on `backend === null`, follow `instructions`."
  Mode 1 (`config`) is unchanged in spirit but the call from inside
  it (currently `aide_init({ category: "mcp", brainPath: <p> })`) now
  writes under the `brain` key automatically (per step 4) — no shim
  edits needed for that. The shim refactor lives in a different
  module from this plan and is not in scope here.

- **Agent frontmatter sweep — `mcpServers: [brain]`.** With the fixed
  `brain` key, every agent definition under `.claude/agents/` and
  `.claude/skills/` that today references `mcp__obsidian__*` tools or
  declares `mcpServers: [obsidian]` becomes a uniform
  `mcpServers: [brain]` rewrite. This is a one-time bulk edit that
  must follow the `aide_brain` ship — agents must keep working
  during the transition, so the rewrite happens after the runtime
  tool is live and tested. Track as a separate task.

- **Migrate the `obsidian` orphan key cleanup.** Step 4b emits
  `would-overwrite` when both `brain` and `obsidian` keys are present,
  but does not delete the `obsidian` orphan. A follow-up cleanup
  plan removes the `obsidian` key from `mcpServers` after the
  `brain` write succeeds. Holding this for a separate plan keeps
  the migration step's scope tight (one write, one prescription)
  and avoids interleaving delete-on-overwrite logic into
  `applySteps`.

- **Update `aide_info` description to point at `aide_brain` for
  runtime usage prose.** Today `aide_info`'s description says nothing
  about the runtime brain tool. A one-line addition ("for runtime
  brain reach prose, call `aide_brain` on demand") helps agents that
  read only the tool list at handshake time. Not a blocker for this
  plan — the agent learns about `aide_brain` from its own description.
